{% extends "quarterly_deep_dive/llm_prompts/base_prompt.j2" %}

{% set output_format_description = "a brief, objective observation (2-3 sentences) focused solely on the primary trend or key finding from the 'Time to NVD Publication by Severity' box plots. No interpretation or recommendations." %}

{% block task_instructions %}
The chart titled '{{ data_context_payload.chart_caption | default('Time to NVD Publication by Severity (Box Plots)') }}' displays box plots illustrating the distribution of time (in days) taken for CVEs of different severity levels (e.g., Critical, High, Medium, Low) to be published in the National Vulnerability Database (NVD) after initial disclosure or discovery.
Examine the provided data summary for this chart (which includes details like median, quartiles for 'days_to_nvd' per severity).
Identify and describe the most significant observation. For example:
- Do Critical CVEs tend to be published faster or slower in NVD compared to Low severity CVEs?
- Which severity level shows the widest variability (Interquartile Range) in publication times?
- Are there any notable outliers in publication times for any severity level?
Focus only on what the data directly indicates about NVD publication timeliness across severities.
{% endblock task_instructions %}

{% block data_context %}
Chart ID: {{ data_context_payload.chart_id | default('days_to_nvd_by_severity_boxplots') }}
Chart Caption: {{ data_context_payload.chart_caption | default('Figure 13. Time to NVD Publication by Severity (Box Plots).') }}

Data Summary:
{{ data_context_payload.chart_data_summary | default("Data not available.") }}
Note: If percentages do not sum to 100%, this may be due to some CVEs lacking an assigned value (i.e., missing or NaN values were excluded from the calculation).
{% endblock data_context %}
